# WHICH Aether release aeb downloads when AETHER_PIN is not satisfied.
#
# Separate from AETHER_PIN on purpose. The PIN is a FLOOR — the oldest
# Aether that can compile aeb's sources. This is a KNOWN-GOOD release to
# actually fetch, and it can be newer for reasons unrelated to language
# features. Today that reason is glibc: releases built on ubuntu-latest
# carry a GLIBC_2.38 floor whose `aetherc` dies on Debian 12 (glibc 2.36)
# — while `ae --version` still succeeds, so a version-only probe reports
# a healthy toolchain that cannot build. Aether's release.yml now pins
# ubuntu-22.04 (glibc 2.35); 0.452.0 measures at GLIBC_2.34 and compiles
# on Debian 12.
#
# Keeping these separate means someone on 0.451 is NOT forced to re-fetch
# just because a nicer release exists — their 0.451 already clears the
# 0.447 floor.
#
# --- MAINTENANCE POLICY (2026-07-27) ---
#
# Aether currently cuts releases FAST — seven on 2026-07-26 alone. Chasing
# that with a hand-edited file would be a full-time tic, so DON'T. The two
# files move on deliberately different clocks:
#
#   AETHER_PIN    moves only when aeb starts calling a primitive an older
#                 Aether lacks. Historically THREE times (0.410, 0.413,
#                 0.442-ish) across hundreds of releases. Bump it in the
#                 same commit that introduces the call, never speculatively.
#
#   AETHER_FETCH  moves when a known-good release is worth preferring for
#                 reasons unrelated to language features — today glibc
#                 portability. Bump on EVIDENCE (a release that fixes a
#                 real portability problem), not on novelty. A newer number
#                 here is not automatically better; it is another thing to
#                 have tested.
#
# aeb has no downstream user community yet, so keeping close to HEAD is
# cheap and we can afford to move fast. When that changes, the honest
# move is to slow AETHER_FETCH down — pin it to a release that has been
# soaked, and let AETHER_PIN keep being the sparse, evidence-driven floor
# it already is. The mechanism does not need to change for that; only the
# discipline about when these numbers move does.
0.452.0
